{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789472900,"nonce":"9LL8y67TGl0/0YlJuXM+EbYC2ys=","title":"rt seam: a host's state per stack, and a run set aside for another context","message":"Under a host (caribou issue 65c83b3) two things are one set per thread that belong to a stack or a context:\n\n1. A host keeps state per stack, a trap chain of frames above all; wren_lift switches stacks itself on `Fiber.call` into a fiber with a krio stack and the return from it, and the host's state stayed the calling stack's meanwhile: a Haxe try open on the calling fiber was the live chain while the called fiber ran. A `stack_switch(from, to)` slot, told before the switch and the other way after the return, lets the host swap.\n\n2. The view's run (`fiber`, `sync_entry_fiber`, `error_fiber`, `has_error`, `last_error`, `pending_fiber_action`) and the JIT's per-thread state are one set per view, while a host's world runs several contexts on the thread, each of which may be inside a Wren call that parked. `VM::set_aside` sets the run aside under an id and leaves the view in no run; `take_up` restores it; the runs set aside are roots of the view meanwhile.\n\nAlso `live_regs::count_on`/`truncate_on`, for a host whose guard a throw lands in with interpreter activations abandoned between: their register files stayed registered and were read after the frames were gone.","files":null}]}